and there's plenty of news to tell you  about this morning tax investigators in  New York State are looking into a  bombshell report that appears to  undermine four decades of self-promotion  by President Trump the New York Times  investigation says the president  apparently received the equivalent of at  least 413 million dollars from his  father's real estate empire much of it  through tax dodges mr. Trump has always  claimed to be a self-made billionaire it  was a central theme of his 2016 campaign  I got a very very small loan from my  father many years ago I built that into  a massive empire and I paid my father  back that alone The Times calls some of  the tax schemes out right fraud the  president's lawyer says there was no  fraud or tax evasion by anyone Paula  Reid is at the White House with more on  this story Paula good morning this was  big news this is big news the New York  Times says it reviewed over a hundred  thousand financial documents and they  found that in every era of the  president's life his finances were  dependent on his father's wealth as you  just heard the president has long  claimed that a million-dollar loan is  what jump-started his empire but The  Times found that he received at least  sixty million dollars in loans from his  father which he did not pay back in full  and much of his wealth was acquired by  participating in what the Times calls  dubious tax schemes among them mr. Trump  and his siblings set up a sham  corporation to disguise gifts from their  parents and helped his father take  improper tax deductions and help  formulate a strategy to undervalue his  parents real estate holdings in all the  times estimates those maneuvers helped  the Trump family avoid paying at least  five hundred and fifty million dollars  in gift and inheritance taxes they  instead paid just over 50 million  dollars but an attorney for the  president called The Times allegations  of fraud and tax evasion 100 percent  false and highly defamatory the White  House issued an unusual statement they  called the article a misleading attack  but then went on to list the president's  many accomplishments and take some shots  at the New York Times now the statute of  limitations for any criminal charges has  passed if these allegations turn out to  be true but there is a possibility of  civil fines  John all that Thanks
 

